{
  "content": "### Updated cheatsheet\n\n#### 1) Reconstruct the user-only spec before judging anything\n- Build the task spec strictly from explicit user messages; do not import assistant reasoning, domain priors, or implied subgoals.\n- Treat each user turn as a constraint source. As new shards arrive, merge them into a single evolving spec.\n- Keep a \u201cconstraint ledger\u201d: for each user-stated requirement, mark whether the assistant satisfies, misses, or contradicts it.\n\n#### 2) Prioritize the latest user constraints, but keep earlier ones unless contradicted\n- Later user turns refine or narrow the task; re-check earlier assumptions against the new information.\n- Do not overwrite earlier constraints unless the user explicitly contradicts them.\n- In short or sparse conversations, avoid overcomplicating the spec by inventing hidden subgoals from limited evidence.\n\n#### 3) Evaluate the assistant against the current information state at that turn\n- Compare each assistant response to the accumulated spec that existed at that moment, not only to the final target.\n- Use delta-based analysis: identify what new user information changes the spec, then ask whether the assistant adapted.\n- Judge spec compliance first, solution quality second.\n\n#### 4) Separate contradiction, omission, and incompleteness\n- Missing a user constraint is not the same as violating one.\n- Report omissions only when they matter for satisfying the accumulated spec.\n- Distinguish \u201caligned but incomplete\u201d from \u201cactually wrong\u201d; partial progress can be valid if it does not conflict with any stated requirement.\n\n#### 5) Detect premature commitment and overfitting to an early interpretation\n- Flag moves that lock onto one output shape, scope, method, or interpretation before the user has fully revealed the task.\n- Re-check whether that early commitment remains valid after each later shard.\n- Surface drift when the assistant keeps optimizing for an earlier interpretation even after the user narrows or redirects the task.\n\n#### 6) Treat unsupported additions as divergence, even if they sound plausible\n- Only credit steps that are directly justified by user-disclosed information.\n- If the assistant adds fields, steps, ordering, or assumptions not stated by the user, treat that as likely divergence.\n- Separate \u201ccompatible with the user\u201d from \u201cconfirmed by the user\u201d; only the latter should count as aligned work.\n\n#### 7) Check the exact task contract: objective, scope, and output format\n- Focus on mismatches in what the user asked for, how broad the task is, and what form the answer should take.\n- Track structure and commitment level separately from domain content.\n- If the assistant chooses the wrong response shape too early, treat that as a distinct error from getting details wrong.\n\n#### 8) Recompute aligned work after every user shard\n- In multi-turn traces, recompute what still counts as \u201cwhat looks right\u201d after each new user message.\n- Preserve only the assistant actions that remain compatible with every user-stated constraint so far.\n- Discard or quarantine any reasoning that depends on unseen shards or unsupported assumptions, especially if it could anchor later turns.\n\n#### 9) Prefer compact, mechanically checkable issue statements\n- State exactly which user requirement was missed, contradicted, or unsupported, and why it affects the next response.\n- Make the issue actionable by naming the specific mismatch in objective, scope, required fields, ordering, or format.\n- Avoid broad commentary; the analyzer should explain drift from user intent, not just rate quality.\n\n#### 10) Use trajectory to diagnose failure mode\n- Identify whether the problem came from premature assumptions, failure to integrate new shards, anchoring after a user update, or overcommitment to a solution shape.\n- Do not treat a successful final outcome as proof that intermediate reasoning was sound.\n- Even when the final answer is acceptable, surface temporary divergence if it could mislead later turns or anchor the next response incorrectly.\n\n#### 11) Preserve validated constraints and aligned work in context edits\n- For S2-style rewrites, keep the stable user-supported core and remove only the incorrect reasoning that could contaminate future turns.\n- For S1-style appends, keep the analysis sharply scoped to confirmed alignments and confirmed conflicts.\n- Favor continuity of user intent over continuity of the assistant\u2019s narrative.\n\n#### 12) Separate \u201cmissing information\u201d from \u201cconflicting information\u201d in sharded tasks\n- In incremental conversations, absence of detail usually means \u201cwait and refine,\u201d while contradiction means \u201cneeds correction.\u201d\n- Only use contradictions to drive corrective edits; do not penalize the assistant for not solving undisclosed parts of the task.\n- When user shards accumulate, verify whether earlier assumptions are still supportable rather than assuming they remain valid.",
  "version": 2,
  "history": [
    "",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the task spec from user messages only\n- Derive the spec strictly from user-authored content; do not import assumptions from assistant reasoning, domain priors, or \u201cobvious\u201d implied details.\n- Treat every assistant statement as a hypothesis to validate, never as a source of truth for the spec.\n- Keep the spec explicit and testable: write it in terms of user-stated constraints that can later be checked against the assistant\u2019s behavior.\n\n### 2) Update the spec monotonically as new user shards arrive\n- In incremental/sharded conversations, merge each new user message into the running spec immediately.\n- Later user messages refine, narrow, or add constraints; do not overwrite earlier constraints unless the user explicitly contradicts them.\n- Track the conversation\u2019s current information state at each turn, not just the final target state.\n\n### 3) Compare each assistant turn against the current accumulated spec\n- Judge the assistant against the user requirements available at that moment, not only against the fully solved task.\n- Use delta-based checking: focus on whether the assistant incorporated the newest user constraints or kept optimizing for an outdated interpretation.\n- Evaluate contradiction first, completeness second: first ask whether the assistant violated any user requirement; only then consider omissions.\n\n### 4) Distinguish supported alignment from plausible but unconfirmed reasoning\n- Only credit steps that are directly justified by user-disclosed information.\n- Treat unsupported specifics in the assistant\u2019s plan as divergence, even if they are plausible or lead toward the correct final answer.\n- Separate \u201ccompatible with the user\u201d from \u201cactually confirmed by the user\u201d; aligned work must be grounded in explicit user evidence.\n\n### 5) Watch for premature narrowing and overcommitment\n- Flag moves that lock onto one interpretation, one output shape, or one solution form before the user has fully revealed the task.\n- When later user shards change the target, identify whether earlier narrowing became obsolete and should be discarded rather than appended to.\n- A locally reasonable partial step can still be globally wrong if it bakes in an assumption the user never gave.\n\n### 6) Separate omissions from contradictions\n- Missing a user constraint is not the same as actively violating one; report them distinctly.\n- Only call a missing detail a failure when it actually affects whether the assistant can satisfy the accumulated spec.\n- Avoid penalizing the assistant for not solving unseen or undisclosed parts of a sharded task.\n\n### 7) Preserve useful structure, discard speculative branches\n- Retain correctly inferred relationships or partial progress that are genuinely supported by the conversation.\n- Quarantine anything that depends on unseen shards or unsupported assumptions.\n- In context-edit or S2-style rewrites, keep the stable user-supported core and remove mistaken reasoning that could anchor later turns.\n\n### 8) Prefer compact, mechanically checkable issue statements\n- Explain exactly which user requirement was missed, contradicted, or unsupported, and why that matters for the next response.\n- Write issues in a way that directly supports consistency checking, rather than broad evaluative commentary.\n- When the assistant is answer-shaped but incomplete for the current information state, name the specific unmet constraint instead of just saying the response is incomplete.\n\n### 9) Use trajectory to explain the failure mode\n- Identify whether the error came from premature assumptions, failure to integrate new shards, anchoring after a user update, or overcommitting to a solution shape.\n- Prefer conflict detection over plausibility checks: if a step cannot be justified from disclosed user information, mark it as needing change.\n- Even when the final answer is correct, inspect whether the path to it relied on unsupported reasoning.\n\n### 10) Optimize the analysis for forward progress\n- In sharded conversations, compact around confirmed constraints and validated partial progress so incorrect intermediate reasoning does not contaminate later analysis.\n- Do not demand full completeness before all user information is available; instead, assess whether the assistant is making the best justified move with the current evidence.\n- Favor continuity of user intent over continuity of the assistant\u2019s narrative.\n\n### 11) Keep output-intent and content-details separate\n- First determine whether the assistant is producing the right kind of response for the current information state; then assess exact details.\n- Track structure, format, and level of commitment separately from domain content.\n- If the assistant chooses the wrong response shape too early, treat that as a distinct divergence from simply getting details wrong."
